WEBVTT

00:00:00.662 --> 00:00:25.694
<v Casey>Hey there, good to have you. It's Sunday, so we're in the Earth Systems and Climate Science stretch. Today we've got Arctic lakes that are getting darker, a Homo naledi cave that may hold an all-female burial site over 240,000 years old, and a Remembrance Day record that Quesnel and Williams Lake both broke. Then the question we'll sit with in the deep dive: what does one warm day actually tell you about climate? Stick around.

00:00:25.794 --> 00:00:44.498
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Casey. It's Sunday, November 15th, 2026. Today's theme is Science, Wonder &amp; the Natural World. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:45.498 --> 00:01:20.074
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We're reading the physical world today, from fossils to Arctic lakes to one record day in two towns. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's light drizzle and around 7 degrees, with a high of 11 and a low of 3 today, and a 10 per cent chance of precipitation. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around 5 and partly cloudy, Williams Lake around 8 and partly cloudy, Quesnel around 8 and mainly clear, and Redstone around 1, also mainly clear. Most of today's news sits close to the ground, so we'll start there.

00:01:21.274 --> 00:01:56.362
<v Casey>Spanish Mountain Gold has redesigned its proposed open-pit gold mine near Likely, about 70 kilometres northeast of Williams Lake. The plan uses filtered stack tailings, also called dry stacking. The company estimates the mine would put $190 million a year into the Cariboo economy. It hopes for a final investment decision in 2028, with 750 to 1,000 jobs during a 24-month construction period, then up to 250 full-time jobs over a 25-year mine life. At a community open house in Williams Lake earlier this month, Mayor Surinderpal Rathor said, "It's mining time."

00:01:56.972 --> 00:02:02.228
<v Riley>Dry stacking means the tailings go into a filtered pile instead of a wet pond behind a dam.

00:02:03.428 --> 00:02:42.580
<v Riley>Satellite work found greening at about 38 per cent of sampled Arctic sites between 1985 and 2016. Over 1984 to 2019, green-wavelength readings fell 15 per cent across more than 400,000 North American boreal and Arctic lakes, which points to darker water. Researchers say thawing permafrost may be darkening the lakes as soil mixes in. Soot from wildfires, oil and gas work, shipping and tourism settles on snow and ice, absorbs sunlight and speeds melting. Darker lakes hold heat longer, and expanding shrubs are shading berry patches that Arctic residents rely on.

00:02:43.011 --> 00:02:45.939
<v Casey>Four hundred thousand lakes, and the cause is still a "may."

00:02:47.139 --> 00:03:14.107
<v Casey>Drought has tightened forage supplies, and advisers are telling cattle producers to match winter rations to forage quality and herd needs. Feed is a major operating expense. South Dakota State University research found that limit-fed, energy-dense diets cut feed use and methane emissions without harming calf growth through weaning, saving about $29 per cow. Kansas State cow-calf specialist Jason Warner advises comparing protein supplements by cost per unit of protein.

00:03:15.307 --> 00:03:47.987
<v Riley>Ancient proteins recovered from Homo naledi fossils in South Africa's Rising Star Cave suggest the cave may hold an all-female burial site more than 240,000 years old. Researchers found no male-specific markers among at least 20 individuals. The study appears in the journal Cell. Homo naledi lived roughly 335,000 to 241,000 years ago. If the reading holds, it would be the earliest known burial place reserved for one sex by a human relative other than Homo sapiens.

00:03:48.395 --> 00:03:52.155
<v Casey>Pulling proteins from teeth is what made the question answerable at all.

00:03:53.355 --> 00:04:27.787
<v Casey>A new study dates the Taung Child, a South African hominin skull found in a limestone quarry in 1924, to between 2.96 and 3.03 million years ago. The earlier estimate was 2.58 million. The skull became the type specimen for Australopithecus africanus, and the authors now argue that fossils grouped under that name may represent more than one species. Fossil pollen points to a wetter, more wooded setting than the area has today. The team links repeated shifts between wet and dry climate to habitats that sometimes isolated populations and sometimes brought them into contact.

00:04:28.987 --> 00:05:03.435
<v Riley>An analysis of 540 million years of marine fossils maps where the record is thin, so researchers know where to dig next. Thomas A. Neubauer of the Bavarian State Collections of Natural History and Mathias Harzhauser of the Natural History Museum in Vienna built the tool. Fossilization is rare, and a fossil only turns up where the rock layers holding it are reachable today. Some periods and regions have been picked over. Others remain largely unexplored, and some of the best odds for new species sit in relatively recent geological periods.

00:05:03.823 --> 00:05:06.759
<v Casey>A map of gaps is a map of where nobody has looked yet.

00:05:07.959 --> 00:05:38.903
<v Casey>Scaphokogia huachafo was an extinct pygmy sperm whale from what is now southern Peru, living 6.3 to 5.6 million years ago. Its skull shows the most extreme facial asymmetry documented in a cetacean, with both bony nostrils on the left side. The team compared 31 whale species using 3D facial measurements, and this genus ranked as the most asymmetric. The skull has closed sutures and no fractures, so the researchers think the distortion was biological. They propose it used echolocation to find prey buried in seabed sediment.

00:05:39.304 --> 00:05:42.464
<v Riley>Both nostrils on one side. Evolution never promised symmetry.

00:05:43.664 --> 00:06:21.216
<v Riley>An Italian-led research team recorded a bull named Achilles mounting a male donkey named Patroclus 51 times over eight days in March 2026, on an Ethiopian plateau. Thirty-four of those mountings involved penetration. The pair licked, rubbed and nuzzled one another, and kept seeking each other out even with other potential mates around. Herders later moved them to separate pastures for the dry season, and researchers hope to arrange a reunion. The findings appeared Nov. 2 in Current Biology. The team says one domesticated pair cannot show why it happened or what the animals felt.

00:06:22.415 --> 00:06:34.951
<v Casey>Genetic evidence indicates that what scientists long treated as one widespread gentoo penguin species is actually four separate species. Kottke.org, quoting the finding, calls it the first new penguin species in 100 years.

00:06:36.151 --> 00:07:02.295
<v Riley>MIT researchers found that adults aged 60 to 84 had significantly weaker waves of cerebrospinal fluid during sleep than adults aged 18 to 39. That fluid helps clear waste from the brain. The team used brain imaging and electroencephalography, and linked much of the gap to weaker deep-sleep brain waves and smaller blood-flow responses in older participants. The rest of the gap remains unexplained.

00:07:03.495 --> 00:07:31.183
<v Casey>Ars Technica reports on a Science study led by Milan Allan of Ludwig Maximilian University of Munich and Ingmar Swart of Utrecht University. The team listened to the faint electrical crackle of current flowing into a copper-based superconductor, a cuprate. Cuprates carry current without resistance at temperatures reachable with liquid nitrogen. The open question is the pseudogap, a state where some electrons seem to vanish. After nearly 40 years, physicists still disagree on why.

00:07:31.877 --> 00:07:36.829
<v Riley>Liquid nitrogen is the affordable part. The missing electrons are the expensive question.

00:07:38.029 --> 00:08:11.413
<v Riley>An independent reanalysis of Event Horizon Telescope data, published Nov. 11 in Nature, reproduced the bright ring around the shadow of M87*. The team, led by CSIRO astronomers Samuel Lai and Nithyanandan Thyagarajan, used a separate imaging method on observations from 2017, 2018 and 2021. The ring averages 42.2 microarcseconds across, consistent with a black hole of about 6.5 billion solar masses. Finer details, like ring thickness and brightness, still shift with the method.

00:08:11.754 --> 00:08:15.178
<v Casey>Two methods, one ring. The thickness still wobbles, and they say so.

00:08:16.378 --> 00:08:43.426
<v Casey>ESA's Hera spacecraft has begun its close study of the Didymos asteroid pair. NASA's DART spacecraft struck the smaller moonlet, Dimorphos, in September 2022 as a deflection test. Hera's images, taken from about 56 kilometres away, show boulders, craters and a sharply defined ridge around Didymos's equator, a side DART never saw. Two CubeSats will follow up on Dimorphos at closer range. ESA plans to release detailed images of both asteroids on November 25th.

00:08:44.626 --> 00:09:08.842
<v Riley>NASA's Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory reentered the atmosphere and burned up over North Sumatra on November 4th, ending a mission of nearly 22 years. Its millions of images remain available for research. In one 24-hour ultraviolet exposure of the Andromeda galaxy, Swift detected about 20,000 ultraviolet sources, many of them hot stars and star clusters that visible-light views hide.

00:09:10.042 --> 00:09:34.178
<v Casey>ESA released Euclid's first major data set, DR1-Foundation, on November 12th. It holds 1.7 petabytes from 13 months of observations, including spectra for 160 million sources. More than 2,500 researchers use it. The telescope sits about 1.5 million kilometres from Earth, and turning its raw images into usable measurements takes careful calibration as the instruments change over time.

00:09:34.521 --> 00:09:40.233
<v Riley>1.7 petabytes is about 1,700 terabytes, which is a lot of hard drives to carry.

00:09:41.432 --> 00:10:00.840
<v Riley>Scientists are focused on roughly the 200 million years after conditions for life emerged on Earth and before its most ancient single-celled ancestor appeared. Quanta reports that after decades of arguing about which molecules came first, a newer generation of researchers is rethinking what life was, is, and could be.

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:15.016
<v Casey>The Atlantic's Toby Lester writes that archaeologists are uncovering evidence of worlds long submerged, which he calls Aquaterra. The piece argues that answers to some of the greatest mysteries of human history lie beneath the ocean.

00:10:16.216 --> 00:10:47.984
<v Riley>Simulations based on Voyager 2 data suggest that electrical conductivity rising with depth inside Uranus and Neptune could explain their tilted, off-centre magnetic fields. Voyager 2 measured a 59-degree tilt for Uranus and 47 degrees for Neptune, with both fields offset from the planets' centres. The model, led by Chi Yan of the University of Texas at Austin and published Nov. 3 in Communications Physics, says the fields could shift substantially over decades, unlike Earth's steadier tilt.

00:10:48.639 --> 00:11:01.487
<v Casey>One flyby per planet, and a field that may move over decades. Somebody will be waiting a while. From distant planets back to a much closer kind of watching: a volunteer group that gets Williams Lake kids looking at the wildlife around them.

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:13.386
<v Riley>Quick meta moment before we move on. Saturdays used to be the week's wrap, but the show now pulls the week's local stories back up on the local day, so you'll hear the Cariboo threads again.

00:11:13.948 --> 00:11:24.436
<v Casey>And you should be careful about calling that a recap. It's recall, which means the show is choosing what to revisit, and that choice is made by the same machinery that picked the stories the first time.

00:11:25.006 --> 00:11:30.214
<v Riley>Fair, but the point is you stop losing local news to the Monday scroll. Worth doing, I'd say.

00:11:30.875 --> 00:11:36.939
<v Casey>Worth doing, yes. I'll just want to hear whether a local story actually gets a second look or just gets replayed.

00:11:37.613 --> 00:11:43.413
<v Riley>Next one: niche interests now close the roundup about once a week. Odd little hobbies, not the headlines.

00:11:43.925 --> 00:11:51.069
<v Casey>Which I'll admit I like. But "about once a week" is doing a lot of work there. Some weeks it won't happen, and I don't think that's a bug.

00:11:51.432 --> 00:11:55.472
<v Riley>Agreed, it's a closer, not a schedule. Which is maybe the right word for it.

00:11:56.050 --> 00:12:04.666
<v Casey>Now the blocklist fix. It was matching the letters MAGA inside the word Magazine, so every magazine outlet was getting dropped. Whole-word matching fixes that.

00:12:05.236 --> 00:12:10.364
<v Riley>Which is embarrassing, because we'd been quietly missing a pile of outlets for who knows how long.

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:19.518
<v Casey>Hard to say what we missed. The fix is solid, but I'd flag that whole-word matching only catches what's spelled out cleanly, so odd spellings can still slip past.

00:12:20.075 --> 00:12:25.235
<v Riley>Good caveat. Last one: four new super-cycle foci, journalism, broadband, environment, health.

00:12:25.855 --> 00:12:34.255
<v Casey>Broadband I'm glad to see, it's the thing this region has been waiting on. My question is whether those foci get real airtime or just show up as labels.

00:12:34.617 --> 00:12:38.833
<v Riley>That's the test. I'd rather we pick a few and follow them than tag everything.

00:12:39.488 --> 00:12:43.752
<v Casey>Fine by me. Let's see who we're actually talking to over the next few weeks, then.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:45.832
<v Riley>Right, let's get into the rest of the show.

00:12:46.504 --> 00:13:10.048
<v Casey>Scout Island Nature Centre is run by volunteers through the Williams Lake Field Naturalists. It's a nature education centre with birding, kids' programs and Salmon in the Classroom, where students follow fish from egg to release. If you've wanted to put names to the birds at the marsh, or you've got a child who keeps asking what that track in the mud was, it's a good place to start. You can find them at scoutisland.ca.

00:13:10.696 --> 00:13:15.232
<v Riley>Volunteer-run means the knowledge stays local, and a salmon program needs exactly that.

00:13:15.901 --> 00:13:20.893
<v Casey>Now to Remembrance Day's warmth in Quesnel and Williams Lake, and what one day can tell us.

00:13:21.393 --> 00:13:42.641
<v Riley>Quesnel hit 18.8 degrees on Remembrance Day. Williams Lake reached 15.5. Environment and Climate Change Canada numbers, reported by the Quesnel Cariboo Observer and CFJC Today, beat the old November 11th records of 16.7 in Quesnel and 13.2 in Williams Lake. Both of those older marks were set in 1981.

00:13:43.151 --> 00:13:53.999
<v Casey>People felt it. Jackets came off in November. So what does one warm day actually tell a resident about the climate, and what has to stand behind it before it counts as change and not just weather?

00:13:54.321 --> 00:14:09.425
<v Riley>Start with the record itself. Quesnel's readings go back to 1893. So 1981 is one point in more than 130 years of numbers. A record is a claim against a long run of readings, and the run is the first thing it needs.

00:14:09.605 --> 00:14:10.341
<v Casey>And Williams Lake?

00:14:10.626 --> 00:14:15.738
<v Riley>I'd check the Williams Lake station history for its start date before saying more about it.

00:14:15.918 --> 00:14:17.062
<v Casey>So two towns, one day.

00:14:17.692 --> 00:14:39.964
<v Riley>Two readings from one weather system, and the cause is documented, which helps. Terri Lang, a meteorologist with Environment and Climate Change Canada, attributed the warmth to an atmospheric river. Subtropical air rose over the coastal mountains and dropped its moisture as rain and snow. Then it sank down the eastern slopes into the Interior and warmed as it compressed.

00:14:40.524 --> 00:14:46.060
<v Casey>So the warmth came from the sky, from a storm. That explains the day. It says nothing about a trend.

00:14:46.618 --> 00:15:02.006
<v Riley>Agreed on the day. A weather event tells you what the pattern did on one afternoon. A pattern that repeats for years is what a climate signal looks like. And the margins are real. Quesnel beat 1981 by 2.1 degrees. Williams Lake beat it by 2.3.

00:15:02.006 --> 00:15:05.118
<v Casey>Then I'd look at the province-wide count before the two towns.

00:15:05.707 --> 00:15:24.179
<v Riley>Environment and Climate Change Canada logged between 27 and 32 high-temperature records across B.C. on the 11th, depending on how you tally them. The strongest was Cache Creek at 21.5 degrees, against a 1981 mark of 17. That's a margin of four and a half degrees.

00:15:24.747 --> 00:15:32.419
<v Casey>Depending on the tally. Nobody puts that part in the headline. Somebody chose which stations count, and somebody chose the cutoff for a record.

00:15:32.933 --> 00:15:51.045
<v Riley>Which is why I'd rather read the method than the headline. The station data is public. And the part that interests me most is the shared benchmark. Quesnel, Williams Lake and Cache Creek all had their old November 11th records from the same year, 1981, and all three fell on the same day.

00:15:51.718 --> 00:16:02.758
<v Casey>That cuts my way too. If one warm day in 1981 set the bar in three towns, then a warm November 11th happens. It happened 45 years ago, before anybody was debating this on a podcast.

00:16:03.378 --> 00:16:14.762
<v Riley>It did. And this one cleared that bar in all three places at once, by two to four and a half degrees. One storm in 1981, one storm in 2026, and the second one ran warmer.

00:16:15.418 --> 00:16:20.562
<v Casey>Two storms is two storms. You'd need to know what the November 11ths in between looked like.

00:16:21.074 --> 00:16:27.018
<v Riley>Which brings us to the bigger issue with any record: what you can say depends on where and how long somebody looked.

00:16:27.351 --> 00:16:30.551
<v Casey>Station placement. Somebody decided where the thermometer went.

00:16:31.114 --> 00:16:51.826
<v Riley>The marine fossil map we had up top works on the same problem at a much bigger scale. Neubauer and Harzhauser didn't just tally what's been found. They charted where the rock is reachable and where nobody has dug. The thin spots are marked, so a researcher knows which conclusions rest on a dense record and which rest on a handful of finds.

00:16:52.183 --> 00:16:55.711
<v Casey>So the map shows you what you don't know. You still don't know it.

00:16:56.397 --> 00:17:08.837
<v Riley>You know how much to trust each part. A record with its gaps marked is more useful than one that pretends to be complete. A weather station could carry the same thing: years missing, sensors changed, sites moved.

00:17:09.466 --> 00:17:17.690
<v Casey>And who draws that map for a thermometer in Quesnel? The fossil team had a reason to chart the holes. A weather station's job is to report the number.

00:17:18.219 --> 00:17:28.131
<v Riley>Environment and Climate Change Canada keeps the station records. The gaps are in there somewhere. Somebody has to ask for them to be published next to the record.

00:17:28.494 --> 00:17:31.022
<v Casey>Somebody always has to ask. Usually nobody does.

00:17:31.579 --> 00:17:46.307
<v Riley>So look at what a record looks like when it's built from many sites over many years. The Arctic lake study is the clearest one we have. Decades of satellite readings across hundreds of thousands of lakes, all measured the same way, all showing darker water.

00:17:46.918 --> 00:17:56.310
<v Casey>Your record day has nothing like that behind it. But I'd still ask who picked the sites in the greening figure. Sites chosen by where satellites pass, or by where a team could reach?

00:17:56.593 --> 00:18:06.121
<v Riley>Whoever picked them, the lake drop comes from one measure, green-wavelength readings, applied across the whole set. One odd lake can't move a number built on that many.

00:18:06.719 --> 00:18:11.911
<v Casey>Unless the odd lakes share a bias. Clustered sites can agree with each other for the wrong reason.

00:18:12.423 --> 00:18:21.551
<v Riley>Then put the clustering question to the authors directly. Clustering might shift the size of the drop. It won't turn darker water into lighter water.

00:18:22.224 --> 00:18:27.624
<v Casey>And the cause is still a "may." The researchers say thawing permafrost may be darkening the lakes.

00:18:28.254 --> 00:18:33.318
<v Riley>The drop is measured. The cause is argued. The measurement holds whichever cause wins.

00:18:33.611 --> 00:18:36.379
<v Casey>I'll take the measurement. I'm not taking the cause on faith.

00:18:36.934 --> 00:18:48.758
<v Riley>Then test the cause. The researchers' proposed mechanism is soil mixing into the water as permafrost thaws. That leaves sediment you can sample, and the answer doesn't depend on any one day's weather.

00:18:49.323 --> 00:19:02.931
<v Casey>Which gets to my real worry. The land swings on its own. The 1981 record says a warm November day happens without anyone forcing it. Wet years, dry years, a storm that comes through warm. That's what makes one record easy to over-read.

00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:22.620
<v Riley>It swings, and that's why the mechanisms matter. An atmospheric river is a known kind of storm. You can name it and you can track how often it comes. Soot on snow, thawing permafrost and darker lakes holding heat are physical processes too. The Arctic research describes them all pushing in one direction.

00:19:23.193 --> 00:19:30.857
<v Casey>Pushing in one direction is a claim you still have to test. Name the soot's sources and you've named the suspects. You haven't measured the push.

00:19:31.385 --> 00:19:42.057
<v Riley>The researchers list the sources: wildfires, oil and gas work, shipping and tourism. Each of those leaves a trail you can count. Fire seasons are logged. Shipping routes are logged.

00:19:42.658 --> 00:19:50.970
<v Casey>Counted where, though? Soot on snow is easy to say. Showing how much of the Arctic it covers takes a long series, which is the thing we keep coming back to.

00:19:51.541 --> 00:19:56.733
<v Riley>Then the mechanisms tell us what to measure, and that's a shorter list than everything.

00:19:57.299 --> 00:20:04.643
<v Casey>Shorter, sure. And one item on it never shows up in a lake count. Expanding shrubs are shading the berry patches Arctic residents rely on.

00:20:05.169 --> 00:20:17.233
<v Riley>A change that shows up in food before it shows up in a chart. That's the version of this a resident notices first. Somebody picking berries knows the patch got thinner long before the paper is published.

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:25.544
<v Casey>Noticing isn't measuring. People remember the warm Remembrance Day this year. Ask them about November 11th, 1981, and you'll get a shrug.

00:20:26.139 --> 00:20:35.771
<v Riley>Fair enough on memory. But noticing tells you where to point the instrument. So bring it home. Quesnel and Williams Lake. What would a local record have to sit beside?

00:20:36.088 --> 00:20:44.112
<v Casey>A long series from the station. Nearby sites that agree. Two towns agreeing on one afternoon isn't the test, because they were under the same storm.

00:20:44.647 --> 00:21:03.687
<v Riley>Then three things. A local series of November highs that points the same direction, from more than one station. A neighbour, like Cache Creek or 100 Mile House, whose series agrees. And a physical process that would explain it if the series holds, like how often atmospheric rivers now reach the Interior.

00:21:04.335 --> 00:21:18.879
<v Casey>Add the station history. Stations move. Sensors get swapped. A thermometer that moves from a field to beside a parking lot reads warmer, and the record looks broken for reasons nobody wrote down. The move log is the first document I'd ask for, and residents never see it.

00:21:19.419 --> 00:21:29.419
<v Riley>Get the move log and the sensor history, and the argument gets shorter. Quesnel's 130-odd years would let you check every move against the readings around it.

00:21:30.057 --> 00:21:43.017
<v Casey>Shorter, not finished. Without the old record's year and size next to the new number, a resident can't tell a fraction of a degree from a big margin. Two degrees over 1981 is a real margin for one day. It still isn't a series.

00:21:43.678 --> 00:21:49.646
<v Riley>Which is why the day matters. It gives people a reason to ask for the series while they still remember the afternoon.

00:21:50.218 --> 00:21:56.906
<v Casey>Nobody asks for the series when the headline runs. They ask when the record breaks again, and by then the first one is forgotten.

00:21:57.573 --> 00:22:08.245
<v Riley>Then ask now. What would change my mind about calling it weather is a run of matching records over decades in Quesnel, Williams Lake and the towns around them. One day wouldn't do it.

00:22:08.425 --> 00:22:09.329
<v Casey>How many decades?

00:22:09.642 --> 00:22:14.202
<v Riley>I'd want a climatologist to give the number, with the station history in hand.

00:22:14.533 --> 00:22:17.685
<v Casey>And I'd distrust anyone who gives you one off a single record day.

00:22:17.975 --> 00:22:20.111
<v Riley>Then the number stays open, and we say so.

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:31.208
<v Casey>Which leaves the part that bothers me most. Who says so publicly when the answer is murky? The paper reports the number. The station issues it. Nobody's job is to say the series isn't long enough yet.

00:22:31.824 --> 00:22:42.520
<v Riley>Lang named the storm and the mechanism, and stopped there. She explained the day and made no claim about the decade. That's what you want from the people holding the instruments.

00:22:43.098 --> 00:22:48.298
<v Casey>Restraint from the forecaster. Then the next storm, the next headline, and nobody connecting the two.

00:22:48.808 --> 00:23:05.232
<v Riley>So the fix is a station record that's public, current and explained. Quesnel's goes back to 1893. Ask for the rest of it, and ask for the old benchmark's year printed beside the new one. Then a warm Remembrance Day becomes one row in a table you can actually read.

00:23:05.792 --> 00:23:25.128
<v Casey>And until that table exists, a warm day is a warm day. I'm not convinced any record tells us more yet. If you've got a station log, a move history or your own backyard thermometer readings from Quesnel or Williams Lake going back years, send them to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a, and we'll see how far back the Cariboo's own notebooks go.

00:23:25.656 --> 00:23:34.648
<v Riley>I'd love to see somebody's grandmother's weather diary from 1981. Thanks for spending your Sunday with us, and hope you had a great weekend.